  "conclusions": [
    "SPY: Spread IS significant univariate predictor (|t|=3.68 > 3.0)",
    "QQQ: Spread IS significant univariate predictor (|t|=3.31 > 3.0)",
    "0050.TW: Spread NOT significant univariate predictor (|t|=2.33 < 3.0)",
    "SPY: Spread adds info beyond VIX (bivariate |t|=3.01 > 3.0)",
    "QQQ: Spread adds info beyond VIX (bivariate |t|=3.15 > 3.0)",
    "0050.TW: Spread DOES NOT add beyond VIX (bivariate |t|=1.95 < 3.0)",
    "SPY: HAR-S does NOT significantly improve over HAR (DM t=1.03)",
    "QQQ: HAR-S does NOT significantly improve over HAR (DM t=0.10)",
    "0050.TW: HAR-S does NOT significantly improve over HAR (DM t=1.86)"
  ],
  "overall_assessment": [
    "OHLC spread has some predictive power for next-day vol (passes Harvey threshold in at least one asset)",
    "Spread provides incremental information beyond VIX in at least one asset",
    "HAR-S does NOT improve over HAR \u2014 spread adds nothing to RV forecasting"
  ],
  "notes": [
    "QLIKE values for spread-only, VIX-only, and spread+VIX models are extremely large for SPY/QQQ because simple linear OLS for r^2 can produce near-zero or negative predictions OOS (clipped to 1e-10), causing QLIKE ratio to explode. This is a known instability of unconstrained linear vol forecasts — the Spearman rank correlations and IS t-statistics are the reliable metrics here.",
    "HAR and HAR-S models have sensible QLIKE because their RV-based regressors keep predictions well-anchored in positive territory.",
    "The IS t-statistics use Newey-West HAC standard errors (lag=10) for robustness to heteroskedasticity and autocorrelation.",
    "All predictors are lagged by 1 day (shift(1)) to prevent lookahead bias."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Corwin-Schultz spread is estimated from daily OHLC, which is a noisy proxy for true bid-ask spread",
    "r^2 (squared daily return) is a very noisy proxy for true volatility — 5-min RV would be better",
    "Simple linear OLS is used; nonlinear models (e.g., log-linear, quantile regression) might capture spread-vol relationship better",
    "IS period (2010-2020) includes post-GFC low-vol era; OOS (2020-2026) includes COVID and its aftermath",
    "No transaction cost or implementability analysis — this is pure statistical predictability"
  ]
}